{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}How ranking works — extdir{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}The exact formula extdir uses to order extensions, rendered from the code that runs it.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
        <h1>How ranking works</h1>
        <p>
            The numbers below are read directly from the constants that do the ranking. This page
            cannot drift out of date, because there is no second copy of the formula to fall behind.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>The formula</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="data">
                <thead>
                <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weight</th><th>What it measures</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                {% for label, row in weights %}
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{{ label }}</strong></td>
                        <td class="num">
                            {{ (row[0] * 100)|round }}%
                            {# The bar makes the shape of the formula visible; the
                               number stays because a weight is a fact, not a vibe. #}
                            <span class="weight-bar" style="width: {{ (row[0] * 100)|round }}%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                        </td>
                        <td class="muted">{{ row[1] }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2>What is deliberately not in it</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Stars and forks do not affect ranking.</strong> In this ecosystem popularity is a
            misleading signal: an excellent extension built by a German agency for its own clients may
            have a dozen stars, while an abandoned experiment has hundreds. Ranking on popularity would
            bury precisely the extensions merchants most need to find. Star counts are still shown, and
            you can sort by them if you want to — they simply do not feed the score.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>There is no manual placement.</strong> No paid slots, no featured section, no
            editorial override, no per-vendor adjustment anywhere in the code. Extensions published by
            the person who operates extdir carry a visible disclosure badge and are scored by exactly
            this formula, with no exception in the codebase for them. There is an automated test that
            fails the build if vendor-specific logic ever appears in the ranking path.
        </p>

        <h2>How compatibility confidence is graded</h2>
        <p>
            A declared version constraint is the maintainer's own statement, and some statements say
            more than others. Where an extension supports the current Shopware release
            {% if currentVersion %}(currently <strong>{{ currentVersion.majorMinor }}</strong>){% endif %},
            the strength of that claim is graded:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="data">
                <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Multiplier</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>explicit</td><td><code>~6.6.0</code>, <code>&gt;=6.5 &lt;6.7</code></td>
                    <td class="num">1.0</td>
                    <td class="muted">Bounded inside one major. A deliberate statement about which versions were considered.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>caret</td><td><code>^6.5</code>, <code>~6.6</code></td>
                    <td class="num">0.85</td>
                    <td class="muted">Expands to <code>&lt;7.0</code>, so it silently claims every later 6.x release — including ones published after the code was last touched.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>wildcard</td><td><code>*</code>, <code>&gt;=6.0</code></td>
                    <td class="num">0.4</td>
                    <td class="muted">Parses, but asserts nothing useful.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>absent</td><td>no constraint</td>
                    <td class="num">0.2</td>
                    <td class="muted">Nothing declared on any Shopware package.</td>
                </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2>How maintenance is judged</h2>
        <p>
            Not by the calendar. A small, finished extension can sit untouched for two years and work
            perfectly — what actually breaks it is a new Shopware release. So activity is measured
            against Shopware's release dates:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Current</strong> — committed to since the newest Shopware minor shipped.</li>
            <li><strong>Lagging</strong> — quiet since then, but active after the previous minor.</li>
            <li><strong>Dormant</strong> — silent across two or more minors <em>and</em> idle for over two years.</li>
            <li><strong>Abandoned</strong> — the maintainer marked it abandoned on Packagist. Their statement, not our inference.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The two-year floor matters: Shopware minors have shipped as little as five months apart, and
            without it a fast release cadence would mark healthy extensions dormant purely because two
            version numbers went by.
        </p>

        <h2>Breaking ties</h2>
        <p>
            Every factor above is categorical, so healthy extensions bunch at the top. Time since the
            last commit is therefore folded in as a smooth curve, decaying over roughly
            {{ recencyHalfLife }} days, which is about one Shopware release cycle. Without it the most
            valuable position in the directory would be decided by database row order — which would not
            be a public algorithm in any meaningful sense.
        </p>

        <h2>Changing the formula</h2>
        <p>
            The weights live in a single class in the public repository. Any change that would benefit
            the maintainer's own extensions requires a written rationale in the commit that would hold
            up if a competitor read it. You can check that in the git history.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
